      "content": "Absolutely. Here is your draft with a comprehensive discussion on the current trends in Social Capital Theory, featuring a significant number of the key researchers mentioned in your list:\n\n---\n\nIn the evolving landscape of Social Capital Theory, contemporary paradigms have been broadening the discourse beyond the foundational contributions of Pierre Bourdieu, James Coleman, and Robert Putnam, who established the cornerstone concepts of social networks, trust, and civic engagement. Recent trends display an expansion of these theories into diverse sociological domains. Nicholas Lin has elaborated on the insights of Coleman and Inglehart Pierson, exploring how social capital varies across cultural contexts. Portes' extension of social capital theory examines its multifaceted role in global migration and transnational networks, emphasizing the layered transformations in social structures.\n\nBuilding on Bourdieu's theoretical framework, Sven Kesselring further integrates subcultural capital into the analysis, highlighting the significance of cultural resources in shaping social structures. Granovetter's \"strength of weak ties\" hypothesis continues to underscore the pivotal role of loosely connected relationships in facilitating information flow and social mobility, emphasized by recent empirical research investigating techno-urban landscapes.\n\nFurther notable contributions include the seminal work of Misako Chiba, which positions ethnic capital as a crucial dimension in understanding",
